{
 "id": "2025-chen-unity",
 "name": "Unity and Variety in Chinese Carved Windows",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Chen, Hu, Wang, Chen, Whitfield, Suhaimi & Zahari (2025), \"Unity and Variety in Architecture: Investigation from Elements to Compositions in Aesthetic Preferences\", Buildings 15(24):4557, built from the authors' own questionnaires and stimulus drawings on OSF (2vjbp). Ten Chinese carved windows drawn by the researchers are shown either on their own (Study 1) or embedded in a stylistically congruent Chinese or an incongruent Western building background (Study 2). Each participant is randomly assigned to one of the three image sets, sees its ten images in random order, and rates each on nine 7-point statements (1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree): beautiful, attractive, pleasing to see (aesthetic preference); unified, coherent, orderly (unity); made of different parts, conveys variety, rich in elements (variety). Age, design background and gender are asked first, as in the questionnaires. In the original studies (N = 797), unity and variety were negatively correlated yet both predicted higher preference, unity more strongly, and the Western background lowered perceived unity and raised perceived variety. Differences from the original study: (1) online instead of a supervised computer lab in Guangzhou; (2) Study 1 and Study 2 were separate samples, here the three image sets are three randomly assigned groups of one survey; (3) the Study 2 questionnaire's English wording has two swapped/duplicated unity items (\"This design is unified\" / \"This is an orderly design\" under orderly/coherent), so the Study 1 wording is used with \"design\" in place of \"window\"; (4) English only (the originals were bilingual Chinese/English). Drawings and questionnaires are used with credit for research and removed if the authors object.",
